  1171	#### 8.4.1 Attempt identity is scoped to an expander incarnation
  1172	
  1173	A record's address is the hash of its value, so two attempts whose records
  1174	are value-equal are one record, whatever the encoder. A bare per-expander
  1175	counter does not prevent that: two expanders observing one medium, batch,
  1176	member, and call path, each at counter `0`, with one macro and one output,
  1177	would write one record; so would one expander restarted and replaying the
  1178	batch. The governing contract records every attempt separately
  1179	(`yin.vm.macro.md:668-669`), so the attempt identity must be unique per
  1180	attempt across expanders and across executions. It is:
  1181	
  1182	```clojure
  1183	:yin.ledger/attempt [incarnation counter]
  1184	```
  1185	
  1186	- **The incarnation** is a value held in the expander's process state
  1187	  `ctx` (`yin.vm.macro.md:216-223`), as a new field `:incarnation`,
  1188	  threaded through every call exactly as `:alloc` and `:t` are. It is
  1189	  minted by the composition **when it constructs the expander** and is
  1190	  never derived from a batch. Its shape is
  1191	  `{:yin.expander/log <log-medium identity> :yin.expander/start <the
  1192	  log's cursor value at construction>}`: the log medium is where this
  1193	  expander's events go, the cursor value is a transport-minted position
  1194	  that no two constructions against one log share, and a composition with
  1195	  no log medium supplies a unique value of its own choosing under the same
  1196	  uniqueness warranty. Two expanders differ in at least one field; one
  1197	  expander constructed twice differs in `:yin.expander/start`.
  1198	- **The counter** is a per-incarnation attempt counter in `ctx`, advanced
  1199	  once per attempt, initial or nested, successful or failed, exactly as
  1200	  `:alloc` advances (`yin.vm.macro.md:360-365`); admission-failure events
  1201	  advance it too, since they are attempts under §4.1.
  1202	- **A staged retry reuses the identity; a distinct execution receives
  1203	  another.** The expander stages `expand-batch`'s output and log payloads
  1204	  and retries a failed flush from the staged data without re-running the
  1205	  expansion (`yin.vm.macro.md:364-365`, `:765-773`): the record, and the
  1206	  attempt identity inside it, are part of what was staged, so the retry
  1207	  writes the same record to the same address, which is the idempotent
  1208	  re-publication the ledger wants. A restart constructs a new expander,
  1209	  which mints a new incarnation, so replaying a batch after a restart
  1210	  mints new attempt identities and new records: that is a distinct
  1211	  execution and is recorded as one. What separates the two cases is
  1212	  whether `ctx` survived: a retry runs under the `ctx` that produced the
  1213	  staged payload; a restart does not have it.
  1214	
  1215	The parent-record chain of §8.4 is unchanged; it gains uniqueness from the
  1216	attempt identity inside every record.
