  1218	### 8.5 Macro declarations travel beside the tree
  1219	
  1220	The `:macro?` flag is outside the canonical tree (§2.5). What replaces it
  1221	is a **declaration fact bound to an occurrence**, produced by the frontend
  1222	and consumed by the expander at admission. The governing contract admits
  1223	and harvests **every entity in the batch, in batch order, including
  1224	definitions disconnected from the execution root**
  1225	(`yin.vm.macro.md:229-249`). A single rooted tree cannot carry a
  1226	disconnected definition, so the composition between frontend and expander
  1227	is an **ordered batch**, not a tree:
  1228	
  1229	```clojure
  1230	{:yin/batch         [<tree-0> <tree-1> … <tree-n>]   ; canonical trees, one per admitted root or disconnected subgraph
  1231	 :yin/root          i                                ; index of the tree the evaluator runs
  1232	 :yin/declarations  #{[j path :yin.macro/definition] …}   ; the definition at path in tree j is a macro
  1233	 :yin/harvest       [#{[j path] …} #{[j' path'] …} …]}   ; one entry per ORIGINAL definition, in admission order;
  1234	                                                     ;   each entry is the set of [j path] occurrences of that definition
  1235	```
  1236	
  1237	Rules:
  1238	
  1239	- **Harvest order is an explicit catalogue, not a traversal.** The
  1240	  governing contract harvests every definition in the batch **in the
  1241	  order its entities appeared in the admitted datom batch**
  1242	  (`yin.vm.macro.md:242-249`), and it admits any acyclic, internally
  1243	  resolved batch; nothing requires entity order to equal structural
  1244	  preorder, and a root may contain definitions A then B whose entities
  1245	  arrived as B then A. Vector order plus preorder therefore does **not**
  1246	  preserve that contract, and this document does not claim it does. The
  1247	  catalogue `:yin/harvest` is the preserved order: a vector with **one
  1248	  entry per original definition**, in the original admission order, each
  1249	  entry the set of `[j path]` occurrences at which that definition
  1250	  appears in the canonical trees, connected or not. Initial harvest
  1251	  (`yin.vm.macro.md` §3.1 step 2) iterates the catalogue, last wins; it
  1252	  never iterates the trees. For a batch converted from datoms by the §9.1
  1253	  adapter the catalogue is derived from entity order, so the legacy
  1254	  contract is preserved exactly. For a batch a tuple-native
  1255	  frontend emits, the frontend chooses the order it writes; the reference
  1256	  frontends write source order, which is the **declaration order** of
  1257	  `yin.vm.macro.md` §3.2 (`yin.vm.macro.md:298-304`: operands before the
  1258	  body of an immediately applied lambda). That is a new contract for a
  1259	  new producer, not a narrowing of the legacy one, and the two are kept
  1260	  distinct: post-expansion declaration order is what the expander uses
  1261	  for definitions its own output forms (§3.1 step 5), and it stays
  1262	  separate from the admission catalogue.
  1263	- **One original definition, many occurrences, one ordinal.** A legacy
  1264	  batch may hold one definition entity reached through several
  1265	  references: the codec emits a pre-assigned entity once and lets every
  1266	  referring site name it (`v2.cljc:394-411`, `seen-eids`), and
  1267	  `yang.clojure` produces exactly this for a defmacro lambda used at its
  1268	  definition and at call sites (`clojure.cljc:418-437`). Inlining that
  1269	  graph into canonical trees produces several `[j path]` occurrences of
  1270	  one definition. The old harvest and declaration catalogue number the
  1271	  **entity**, giving it one declaration ordinal `k`, and a transformer
  1272	  may fabricate a stand-in that selects a catalogue entry by `k`
  1273	  (`yin.vm.macro.md:254-275`), so the ordinal is observable and
  1274	  duplicating it would be wrong. Therefore a catalogue entry is an
  1275	  **occurrence group**: the definition is harvested once, its ordinal
  1276	  `k` is its index in the catalogue, every occurrence in the group is
  1277	  replaced by a stand-in carrying that same `:decl k` (§3.1 step 3), and
  1278	  the catalogue, not the trees, is the identity of the definition. The
  1279	  adapter derives groups from entity identity; a tuple-native frontend
  1280	  emits singleton groups, one per definition it writes, and if it writes
  1281	  one definition at several places it emits one group naming them all.
  1282	- **The catalogue is validated at admission.** Every occurrence in every
  1283	  group must name a `(yin/def <literal sym> _)` node; every such node in
  1284	  the batch must appear in exactly one group; groups are non-empty and
  1285	  pairwise disjoint; every occurrence in a group must have the same
  1286	  `:literal` symbol and the same value-operand content address, since one
  1287	  original definition cannot have two bodies; every `:yin/declarations`
  1288	  entry must be a member of some group, and a group is declared as a whole
  1289	  (all of its occurrences carry the declaration or none do, which is
  1290	  automatic for a legacy entity with one flag). A violation is an
  1291	  admission failure `{:kind :malformed-input :reason :harvest-catalogue}`
  1292	  naming the entry.
  1293	- **Binding.** A declaration `[j path :yin.macro/definition]` names the
  1294	  `:application` at `path` in tree `j`, and through the catalogue the
  1295	  original definition whose group contains `[j path]`; that node must be
  1296	  `(yin/def <literal sym> <lambda>)`, i.e. operator `[:variable yin/def]`,
  1297	  first operand a `:literal` symbol, second operand a `:lambda`. A
  1298	  declaration whose coordinates do not resolve to such a node is an
  1299	  admission failure, `{:kind :malformed-input :reason
  1300	  :stray-macro-declaration}`, the successor of §3.1 step 1's
  1301	  `:stray-macro-lambda`.
  1302	- **Marks are validated before they are stripped.** A frontend or the
  1303	  §9.1 adapter that converts a map AST carrying `:macro? true` must reject
  1304	  a flagged lambda anywhere but the value operand of a `yin/def` as
  1305	  `:stray-macro-lambda` **at conversion**, before the flag is removed;
  1306	  it may never strip such a flag and emit a plain lambda. A flag in the
  1307	  admitted position becomes the declaration at that tree and path. The
  1308	  quiet case `yin.vm.macro.md` decision 5 exists to prevent is therefore
  1309	  prevented at the same point it is today.
  1310	- **Ordering of harvest.** The catalogue's order; last wins, as §3.1
  1311	  step 2 rules. Vector order and preorder play no part in harvest.
  1312	- **Missing fact.** A `(yin/def sym v)` with no declaration at its
  1313	  coordinates is a plain definition. If `sym` is currently a macro, the
  1314	  plain definition removes it, exactly as §3.1 step 2 rules.
  1315	- **Scope.** Harvest is syntactic and whole-batch, as today; a declaration
  1316	  in an unexecuted branch or in a tree the root never reaches is in force
  1317	  for the batch. Declarations are per batch and never persist beyond the
  1318	  harvest that consumed them; the harvested store is the expander's state.
  1319	- **Redefinition.** Two batches that carry identical trees differ in
  1320	  behaviour only through their declarations, which is the intended
  1321	  separation: content is what the program is; being a macro is what this
  1322	  occurrence of a definition is *for*.
  1323	- **Evaluators see the root only.** The composition hands an evaluator
  1324	  `(nth batch root)` after expansion; a tree outside the root is expander
  1325	  input, never program.
  1326	
  1327	This replacement protocol, not the expander itself, is the blocker
  1328	(§10.5): tuple evaluation without macros needs none of it, and the v2
  1329	expander does not exist yet.
