   248	   batch, in batch order, last wins: `v` a `:macro? true` lambda →
   249	   `assoc` into `:store`; `v` anything else (a plain lambda, a variable, a
   250	   literal) → `dissoc`, so a redefinition as a non-macro stops expansion of
   251	   that name. Harvest is *syntactic* and whole-batch by policy: a definition
   252	   in an unexecuted branch, or after its first use in the same batch, is
   253	   still in force for the whole batch. (Clojure defines a macro only when the
   254	   `defmacro` form executes; this design does not, and says so.)
   255	3. **Replace.** Step 2 also numbers every source macro definition it saw,
   256	   in harvest order, into a batch-local **catalogue** `:declared {k {:name
   257	   sym :lambda ast}}`. Each macro definition node is then replaced by a
   258	   **stand-in** `{:type :yin/macro-defined :name <sym> :decl k}` — an
   259	   internal node type of the expander's working representation, not of any
   260	   evaluator. It carries its identity by *shape and ordinal*, so it survives
   261	   every transformation a macro can apply to it: a macro that receives it
   262	   as an operand and returns, wraps, or reorders it hands back the same
   263	   shape, and nothing in the pipeline needs an eid or `m` to recognise it —
   264	   and two definitions of the same name in one batch remain distinguishable,
   265	   so a macro that keeps one and discards the other keeps *that one's* body.
